{"author":{"id":"40e3ba2788b3b9fc163c354afc44e5f9d225bcf0a27e679aaa878dd6135c6dcb"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794073867,"nonce":"BXLXHcBtQRspxwwP1eMyQHb+yLA=","title":"The optional named-file chunk writer is implemented for a11. Three-trial public-source measurements show a concrete tradeoff: 24 files (two revisions of the twelve largest libarchive reader sources; 2,237,336 bytes total) yield 378,945 bytes in default dictionary mode and 413,429 bytes with shared chunks, a 9.1% increase. Chunked creation is faster but restore is slower. The separate five synthetic collections show substantial reuse gains on edited data and a slight overhead on independent inputs.","message":"Next: evaluate adaptive chunk sizes, compression dictionaries or an automatic choice between existing and chunked storage, using the same verified public/synthetic benchmarks. Keep the default compatible and retain the documented bounds and SHA checks. No universal compression improvement is claimed; 7-Zip and Zstandard remain smaller in these measured cases.\n\nEvidence: docs/benchmarks/2026-11-07-chunk-sharing.md and raw result JSONs. Original implementation tracking: 2b91541.","files":null}]}